Sed - stream editor:

The sed (Stream Editor) command in Linux is a powerful command-line utility used to parse, filter, and transform text data dynamically.
It operates non-interactively, processing text line-by-line using a temporary buffer called the pattern space.

-i: Modifies the file directly and permanently (in-place) instead of just printing changes to the terminal.
-n: Suppresses default line printing so sed only displays what you explicitly request.
-E or -r: Enables Extended Regular Expressions (ERE) for advanced pattern matching.
-e: Allows chaining of multiple sed expressions sequentially in one command line.

***************************** syntax: sed [OPTIONS] 'SCRIPT' [INPUTFILE...] *****************************************

sed '' file.txt -- this will just display the whole file again, acts same as cat

deletion:

sed '2d' file.txt -- does not print 2nd line output to the screen, meaning 2d -- delete 2nd line.
sed -i '2d' file.txt -- delete the 2nd line in place, inside the file.

sed '3,7d' file.txt -- Delete a Range of Lines: Drops lines 3 through 7 inclusive.

sed '5,$d' file.txt -- Delete from Nth Line to the End: Drops line 5 up through the final line

sed '/error/d' file.txt -- Delete Matching Patterns: Erases any line that contains the word "error"

sed '/^$/d' file.txt -- Remove All Blank Lines: Cleans up files by wiping empty lines using regex anchors (^$ matches line start to end with nothing between).

sed '/^#/d' file.txt -- Remove Comments: Strips lines starting with a hash symbol


*******sed does not make changes to file in any way, it just reads file and does operation mentioned in '' feild and displays o/p.**********
**************to makes changes to the actually file use "-i" option, this will edit the file in place

Printing:

sed '2p' file.txt -- prints only 2nd line along with whole file, this is sed's default behaviour.
sed -n '2p' file.txt -- this will only print 2nd line, and "-n" option will supress default behavior sed.

sed -n '10,20p' file.txt -- Print a Range of Lines: Mimics a customized head/tail command for lines 10 to 20.

sed -n '/critical/p' file.txt -- Print Lines Matching Pattern: Functions exactly like the grep utility.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
substitute:

sed 's/fish/dog' file.txt -- this will substitute fish with dog in whole file, but only one occurence in a single line, substitues only 1 fish word with dog in each line found.

sed 's/fish/dog/g' file.txt -- this will substitute all occurences of fish with dog in the whole file.

sed -i 's/fish/dog/g' file.txt -- substitues all the occurences and also edits the file in place

sed 's/apple/orange/2' file.txt --  Replaces only the 2nd instance of "apple" found on each line. -- Replace the Nth Occurrence

sed 's/apple/orange/gI' file.txt -- Finds and replaces "apple", "Apple", or "APPLE" seamlessly. -- Case-Insensitive Match (I)

sed '5s/apple/orange/g' file.txt -- Restrict to Specific Lines: Performs substitution exclusively on line 5.

sed 's|/usr/bin|/usr/local/bin|' file.txt -- Use Custom Delimiters: Useful for changing file paths without escaping backslashes.

Text Insertion and Appending Use Cases:

sed '1i\Header Text' file.txt -- Insert Text Before a Line (i): Inserts "Header Text" directly above line 1.

sed '$a\Footer Text' file.txt -- Append Text After a Line (a): Appends "Footer Text" right below the final line.

sed '4c\This is the new line content' file.txt -- Replace an Entire Line (c): Changes line 4 completely to the newly provided content.

