awk command:

syntax: awk [options] 'script' file.txt/STDIN --piped

or
awk [options] 'pattern { action }' input_file


awk command in Linux is a highly versatile tool, serving as a complete, data-driven programming language designed primarily for text processing, 
data extraction, and reporting. 

-- It processes files or data streams line-by-line, automatically breaking each line into indexed fields.

Variable			Description
$0					The entire current line.
$1, $2, ...			Individual indexed columns (fields).
NF					Number of Fields in the current line.
$NF					The value of the very last field on the line.
NR					Number of Records (total lines processed so far).
FS					Input Field Separator (default: whitespace).
OFS					Output Field Separator (default: space).

eg:

awk does not modify anything, it just processed and prints the o/p 
awk's default delimiter is <space>, you can change the delimiter to make feilds based on any character delimiter.

who | awk '{print $1, $3}' -- Targets the first column (username) and third column (login date), stripping out all intervening data.

awk -F: '{print $1, $NF}' /etc/passwd -- change the default <space> delimiter to ":"
--  The -F: option splits records by colons. This safely isolates the username ($1) and user shell ($NF) from the Linux password file.

regex matching and downstream passing :
awk '/manager/ {print $1, $4}' employees.txt -- Searches sequentially for records containing the string "manager". It prints only the name ($1) and salary ($4) fields for matching rows.

awk -F, '$3 > 5000 {print $1}' sales.csv --  Inspects column 3 numerically. If the value exceeds 5000, it prints the first column.

awk 'NR==3, NR==6 {print NR, $0}' log.txt --  Evaluates the current line count via NR. It outputs lines 3 through 6 inclusive, prepended with their row index.

awk 'BEGIN{FS=" "; OFS=" | "} {print $1, $2, $3}' system.log -- Accepts standard space-delimited text via FS, but formats output elements strictly using pipe anchors via OFS

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Use grep: When you simply want to find lines matching a basic pattern.
Use sed: When you need to make straightforward line-by-line text substitutions or deletions.
Use awk: When your data structure is column-based, requires mathematical aggregation, or depends on state tracking across lines.

