printf command:

does same as echo but better with more control.

-- a powerful utility used to print formatted text, numbers, and variables to the terminal
-- Derived from the printf function in the C programming language, it provides far greater control over layout, precision, and alignment than the standard echo command.
-- Unlike echo, printf does not append a newline character automatically; you must explicitly define how the output is structured.

syntax: printf FORMAT [ARGUMENTS...]
FORMAT: A string containing literal characters, escape sequences, and format specifiers.
ARGUMENTS: The data or variables inserted into the format specifiers.

Format specifiers:

%s: Formats the input as a basic string.
%b: Formats a string and explicitly expands backslash escape sequences (like \n) within that argument.
%d or %i: Formats a signed decimal integer.
%f: Formats a floating-point number.
%x / %X: Formats an unsigned integer as hexadecimal (lowercase/uppercase).
%%: Prints a literal percent sign (%)

Important Escape Sequences:

\n: Creates a newline.
\t: Inserts a horizontal tab space.
\\: Prints a literal backslash.
\a: Triggers an alert (produces a terminal system bell sound).

Eg:

printf "Hello World\n" -- Because printf doesn't append a newline by default, you must explicitly use \n to transition to the next line.

-- Injecting Multiple Variables into Text.  You can cleanly pass shell variables directly into structured text templates
NAME="Alice"
AGE=25
printf "User %s is %d years old.\n" "$NAME" "$AGE"
# Output: User Alice is 25 years old.

Creating Aligned Columns (Data Tables)You can define explicit column widths by placing a number between % and the specifier. 
A positive number right-aligns data, while a negative number left-aligns it.

# Left-align Name (15 chars wide), Right-align Score (5 chars wide)
printf "%-15s %5s\n" "Name" "Score"
printf "%-15s %5d\n" "John Doe" 95
printf "%-15s %5d\n" "Jane Smith" 100


Formatting Floating-Point PrecisionControl the number of decimal points displayed by adding .N before the %f specifier.

PI=3.14159265
printf "Pi to 2 decimals: %.2f\n" $PI   # Output: 3.14
printf "Pi to 4 decimals: %.4f\n" $PI   # Output: 3.1416

